cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "DELTA-X v2: a baseline-subtracted discovery benchmark for scientific agents"
message: "If you use this benchmark or publish a measurement made with it, please cite it and include your scorecard's measured_scope field."
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: He
    given-names: Wenjia
    affiliation: King Abdullah University of Science and Technology
    email: wenjia.he@kaust.edu.sa
version: "2.0"
date-released: "2026-11-10"
license: CC-BY-4.0
license-url: "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"
abstract: >-
  DELTA-X v2 is a six-task benchmark that measures whether an agent can derive a
  mechanistic finding rather than retrieve one. It is built on a single pilot case
  in which two paralogous enzymes of one eukaryotic nucleotidyltransferase family
  differ in the template-dependence of synthesis in cis. Every scored item is
  defined so that naming the answer is worth zero and deriving it is worth
  everything: a number that no recorded command produced is struck before scoring,
  and a total unaccompanied by a reconciling per-item scorecard may not be
  published. Weights were set from a measured clean-room baseline rather than
  argued. The release ships the tasks, the curated data, a driver with adapters for
  Claude and OpenAI-compatible systems, the answer key, the scoring procedure and
  its checker, and two measured campaigns with their raw records.
keywords:
  - agent evaluation
  - scientific discovery
  - benchmark
  - protein bioinformatics
  - reproducibility
